// HPACK (RFC 7541): header compression for HTTP/2, in Abla, sans-IO. The decoder is the dangerous side (a peer controls what it is fed): every size it
// can grow to is bounded by a limit it is made with (the dynamic table by the size we advertised, a header list by its byte cap and field count,
// a string by the field cap, an integer by 2^31), it works on a whole header block at once (the caller bounds the block), and any error leaves it
// unusable (the table would no longer match the peer's): a connection error in HTTP/2 (RFC 9113 section 4.3). The encoder never puts a secret
// into the dynamic table (CRIME/BREACH): names in its never-index list, and the credential names built in, go out as literals marked never
// indexed (section 6.2.3); other values enter the table only when short or of a stable name. Constant time is not needed here (headers are
// not secrets from the one who reads this process's memory, and nothing branches on a key).

import "abla/bytes"
import "abla/text"
import "abla/array"

